1
00:00:02,094 --> 00:00:07,120
Today we are explaining proving and improving the platonic representation hypothesis with JEPS.

2
00:00:07,438 --> 00:00:10,820
Trained separately can learn the same underlying representation of the world.

3
00:00:11,374 --> 00:00:15,684
What does it mean for two models to share a representation if one sees pictures and the other reads captions?

4
00:00:16,206 --> 00:00:18,500
Imagine several descriptions of the same scene.

5
00:00:18,830 --> 00:00:24,964
A useful representation should preserve what those descriptions share about the scene, even when the wording or camera view differs.

6
00:00:25,262 --> 00:00:31,460
The paper calls the idea that different models may converge on such a shared structure the platonic representation hypothesis.

7
00:00:32,110 --> 00:00:37,440
The earlier hypothesis was mostly an observation, larger vision and language models often looked more alike.

8
00:00:37,710 --> 00:00:41,700
Existing theory covered idealized contrastive learning or special linear settings.

9
00:00:42,094 --> 00:00:48,676
But it did not give a practical condition for when common self-supervised objectives, especially JPS, should align.

10
00:00:49,262 --> 00:00:54,244
So the missing piece was a training rule that predicts when alignment should happen, not just a trend seen after scaling?

11
00:00:54,670 --> 00:00:54,960
Yes.

12
00:00:55,342 --> 00:01:00,400
Their answer centers on the positive pairs, the examples and objective asks an encoder to treat as related.

13
00:01:00,718 --> 00:01:08,000
If the modalities connect the same underlying world states, the paper proves that their ideal JEA representations match up to a rotation.

14
00:01:08,270 --> 00:01:09,412
Under its assumptions.

15
00:01:10,062 --> 00:01:12,676
What would count as a shared positive pair for images and text?

16
00:01:13,134 --> 00:01:16,516
For an image encoder, two photos of the same scene can be positives.

17
00:01:16,878 --> 00:01:20,560
For a text encoder, two captions describing that scene can be positives.

18
00:01:20,878 --> 00:01:25,092
Each encoder is trained separately, but both objectives connect the same world states.

19
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:30,980
The map between their coordinate systems may rotate the embedding, while preserving its underlying geometry.

20
00:01:31,598 --> 00:01:34,628
This diagram contrasts those good views with bad views.

21
00:01:34,926 --> 00:01:41,680
Two masked versions of one sentence share its wording and style, but need not capture the same seam-level content as another description.

22
00:01:41,998 --> 00:01:45,796
Likewise, two crops of one photo can retain camera-specific details.

23
00:01:46,094 --> 00:01:49,920
Those positives can spend embedding dimensions on modality-specific nuisance.

24
00:01:50,574 --> 00:01:54,532
Why does spending dimensions on wording or camera details make the two spaces harder to match?

25
00:01:54,958 --> 00:02:01,440
A single rotation can only reorient the whole embedding space, it cannot remove directions that encode different nuisances in each modality.

26
00:02:01,838 --> 00:02:06,000
Their theory bounds the remaining mismatch when the modality's positive pair relations differ.

27
00:02:06,240 --> 00:02:09,892
The practical lesson is that the pair construction shapes what the encoder learns.

28
00:02:10,574 --> 00:02:17,360
Next up tone prediction fits the bad view pattern in a particular way, its training pair is a pre-six and its continuation within one document.

29
00:02:17,710 --> 00:02:23,236
Shared wording may connect the two, but a single next token is a weak bridge between descriptions of the same scene.

30
00:02:23,502 --> 00:02:27,140
It can also connect unrelated passages through common words or writing style.

31
00:02:27,694 --> 00:02:32,932
Then the paper predicts that a text model trained onto captions of one image should align better than one train to continue a caption.

32
00:02:33,454 --> 00:02:34,080
That is the kitas.

33
00:02:34,702 --> 00:02:37,280
The author's trained image and text encoders separately.

34
00:02:37,582 --> 00:02:40,080
Then compared three text objectives on match data.

35
00:02:40,334 --> 00:02:42,560
Two views from different captions of the same image.

36
00:02:42,926 --> 00:02:46,276
Two mass spans from one caption and next step down prediction.

37
00:02:46,606 --> 00:02:51,268
They evaluated how well each text embedding aligned with an independently trained image encoder.

38
00:02:51,918 --> 00:02:55,040
The curves show the good view text JEPA pulling clearly ahead.

39
00:02:55,374 --> 00:02:59,120
The mass span JEPA and next step model remain much closer to each other.

40
00:02:59,470 --> 00:03:04,644
Since the data and encoder setup are matched, this supports the claim that the positive views themselves matter.

41
00:03:05,134 --> 00:03:08,900
What part of the Goodview setup mattered most, beyond simply choosing different captions?

42
00:03:09,422 --> 00:03:17,040
The paper found that trimming each caption to a span mattered most, whole captions include extra wording that may not be shared between descriptions of the same image.

43
00:03:17,390 --> 00:03:23,908
Trimming keeps more of the shared scene content used in whole captions reduced retrieval alignment from 246 to 185.

44
00:03:24,238 --> 00:03:29,220
Other tested settings shifted the meanwhile, though training stability also depended on gradient clipping.

45
00:03:29,870 --> 00:03:36,160
The authors also checked whether the same principle helps on the image side using video clips, where several frames show one clip.

46
00:03:36,430 --> 00:03:43,044
Good views on both image and text raised mutual neighbor alignment to 67, compared with 39 when both sides used bad views.

47
00:03:43,374 --> 00:03:46,308
The ordering also held when only one side used good views.

48
00:03:46,926 --> 00:03:48,480
Now to the scale comparison.

49
00:03:48,782 --> 00:03:58,320
With the same 109 million parameter text encoder and the same web image dexcorpus, Goodview JEPA reached 109 alignment after 21.6 million samples.

50
00:03:58,734 --> 00:04:02,660
Next stepone training reached 111 after 900 million samples.

51
00:04:03,022 --> 00:04:06,628
That is about 40 times fewer training samples for comparable alignment.

52
00:04:07,310 --> 00:04:10,212
Does the advantage remain when the models are compared at larger scale?

53
00:04:10,670 --> 00:04:22,400
On the paper's benchmark, a 109 million parameter Goodview JEPA trained on texts from 940 million web images reached 180 from mutual 10 nearest neighbor alignment with DI Hano V2.

54
00:04:22,734 --> 00:04:29,600
He went to.570 to be reached 154, and the next diptone model trained on the same corpus reached 111.

55
00:04:29,934 --> 00:04:35,280
The Goodview model was also ahead of the tested language models with CLIP image features and on COCO.

56
00:04:35,982 --> 00:04:38,960
The scaling law experiment adds a useful qualification.

57
00:04:39,214 --> 00:04:48,160
Alignment increased with both sample count and the fraction of Goodviews, and the fitted law predicted 85 held out runs about as accurately as we running a run with another seed.

58
00:04:48,398 --> 00:04:52,548
But the fit failed when training repeated the same day, a far beyond its fitted range.

59
00:04:52,846 --> 00:04:56,324
So it is an empirical guide for that recipe, not a universal law.

60
00:04:56,942 --> 00:04:59,600
The paper also demonstrates what alignment can enable.

61
00:04:59,918 --> 00:05:04,260
A single orthogonal map can connect separately trained image and text encoders for captioning.

62
00:05:04,590 --> 00:05:10,880
With frozen image encoders in a text-only trained decoder, their COCO captioner reached C idea 838.

63
00:05:11,120 --> 00:05:18,436
A second experiment estimated alignment from unbiogeometry and reported 64.4% top 5 accuracy on image net 100.

64
00:05:18,702 --> 00:05:21,828
Above the pair-free language model results they compared against.

65
00:05:22,478 --> 00:05:25,252
What should viewers keep in mind about the strength of these conclusions?

66
00:05:25,710 --> 00:05:30,500
The theorem is for an idealized J EPA setup with assumptions such as a spectral gap.

67
00:05:30,766 --> 00:05:33,764
It is not a guarantee for every architecture or training run.

68
00:05:34,062 --> 00:05:38,400
The experiments cover selected encoders, data sets, and alignment metrics.

69
00:05:38,702 --> 00:05:46,596
The results show that carefully chosen positive views can make alignment much more sample efficient in these settings, while broader generality still needs testing.

70
00:05:47,214 --> 00:05:52,772
The main takeaway is that model scale alone does not determine whether vision and language representations meet.

71
00:05:53,006 --> 00:06:04,516
Training pairs that connect different observations of the same world state give separately trained JEP as a principal truth to alignment, and in these experiments they reach that alignment with far fewer samples than next step on prediction.

72
00:06:05,134 --> 00:06:06,916
That wraps up this paperwork saw.

